==French==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm|fr}} {{term|lang=frm|amuser||to amuse, divert, babble}}, from {{etyl|fro|fr}} {{term|amuser|lang=fro||to stupefy, waste time, be lost in thought}}, from {{term|a-|lang=fro}} + {{term|lang=fro|muser||to stare stupidly at, gape, wander, waste time, loiter, think carefully about, attend to}}, of uncertain and obscure origin. Cognate with {{etyl|oc|-}} {{term|musa||idle waiting|lang=oc}}, {{etyl|it|-}} {{term|musare||to gape idly about|lang=it}}. Possibly from {{etyl|fro|-}} *{{term||mus|lang=fro|snout}} from Proto-{{etyl|roa|-}} *{{term||mūsa|snout|lang=la}} (—compare {{etyl|ML.|fr}} {{term|musum|mūsum|muzzle, snout|lang=la}}), of {{etyl|gem|fr}} origin, from {{proto|Germanic|title=Proto-Germanic base|mū-|muzzle, snout|lang=fr}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|mū-|lips, muzzle|lang=fr}}. Compare {{de}} {{term|Maul||muzzle, snout|lang=de}}.

Alternative etymology connects {{etyl|fro|-}} ''muser'' and {{etyl|oc|-}} ''musa'' with {{etyl|goh|fr}} {{term|muoza||careful attention, leisure, idleness|lang=frk}}, from {{proto|Germanic|mōtōn|leave, permission|lang=fr}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|med-|to acquire, possess, control|lang=fr}}. Compare also {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|muozon|muozōn|to be idle, have leisure or opportunity|lang=goh}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Musse||leisure|lang=de}}. More at {{l|en|empty}}.

